3.2 A Detailed Example of Interplay
The following recent example illustrates in more detail the theme that the core research in statistics feeds off and interacts with outreach efforts. Since at least some of the work is NSF funded, it indicates in part the kind of interactions that should be kept in mind when supporting core research. In 2001 three astrophysicists published in Science (Miller, Nichol and Batuski, 2001) a confirmation of the Big Bang theory of the creation of the universe. They studied the imprint of so-called acoustic oscillations on the distribution of matter in the universe today and showed it was in concordance with the distribution of cosmic microwave background radiation from the early universe. It not only provided support for the Big Bang theory, it also provided an understanding of the physics of the early universe that enabled predictions of the distribution of matter from the microwave background radiation forward and backward in time.


