tive models from existing data and their assessment, is of prime importance. The complexity of these problems usually requires the development of novel statistical tools, which in turn generate new problems for theoretical investigation. The application of newly developing statistical methods to the design of traditional experiments with humans will also need careful investigation. Already we see our clinical colleagues’ interest in incorporating new technologies like microarray chips and functional magnetic resonance imaging into their clinical trial protocols. How to balance the promise of these methods with the relatively conservative approach of large samples and simple transparency, valued by both funding and regulatory agencies, will be another challenge. Like the blending of social science with stochastic processes that is taking place in biostatistics with quality-of-life–adjusted survival analysis, methods for marrying the new technologies with the old techniques need to be developed and evaluated critically. Whether funded by the National Institutes of Health or the National Science Foundation, the entire profession of statistics must dedicate itself to these challenges, and we expect we will see a convergence in common interests at these agencies. New models of education are required to meet the research needs outlined in the report. Graduate education is basic to the achievement of national goals in at least two ways. First, universities are responsible for producing the teachers and researchers of the future. Second, graduate education contributes directly to the broader national goals of technological, economic and cultural development. The report highlights strains on the statistical program which come from the fact that statistics is an infrastructural science. Thus we need to produce more versatile statisticians by providing more options than are currently available to allow students to gain a wider variety of skills than they now possess. Versatility can be promoted in both academic and career skills. At the same time, care should be taken so that important goals are not compromised, including a comprehensive understanding of the underlying scientific principles on which the field of statistics is based, and acquisition of the appropriate computing and mathematical tools. A deep understanding of at least one subject-matter area must also be a part of a statistician’s training. The research and education needs demand that appropriate resources be allocated to ensure that these needs are met. We were surprised by the statement,
“We are, in a sense, victims of our success.” Although the measure of the “number of research proposals received in a scientific area” may be used as an indicator of growth, it is a misleading measure on at least two accounts. One is that growth in certain areas does not happen in a vacuum. In many cases it is proportional to investments made and priorities set. The other is the implication of a very narrow definition of program director productivity, which does not take into account the collaborative endeavors of program directors. Statistics, by its nature, is a collaborative scientific discipline; thus the collaborative relationships with other scientific disciplines within the NSF and in the community at large, fostered by the program directors, are critical for the continued growth and vitality of the field of statistics. These relationships are not formed overnight and need to be nurtured if they are to be successful. Therefore even from this perspective, the issue of resource allocation is indeed critical. The report provides several recommendations for the field of statistics and its key stakeholders. In our opinion, it is imperative that the statistics community rally behind the report to ensure that the proposed recommendations are implemented. These recommendations should be considered a positive first step toward successfully handling the challenges that the field of statistics faces as it ventures into the twenty-first century.
