Comment
Leo Breiman
At the top, the authors of the report and I agree on what is central in statistics: “Its over-arching goal is the extraction of scientifically useful information from data of all types.” Then the report seems to lose sight of this goal and talks about core activities such as unifying concepts and asymptotic approximations. Looking at the August 2003 issue of Statistical Science with its tribute to John Tukey, I was reminded again of how unusual John was, and I wondered how he would react to this report. John never had a formal course in statistics. He learned his statistics while solving war-related problems during World War II. He seems to have taken great delight in new problems and opportunities for invention. Regarding his attitude, here is an excerpt from his conversation:
Many of the real innovations were stimulated by real data sets . . . the relative frequency of stimulation is often quite low—But I am sure that I would have found it much harder and less rewarding to sit in a pure theorist’s ivory tower.
John’s best innovations came as responses to problems where new methods had to be invented to deal with the data. This is true throughout the history of statistics: Least squares to handle astronomical data in the early 1800s; the Gaussian distribution to quantify the error distributions; data on the inheritance of traits led to correlation and regression; Fisher’s position at an agricultural station led to analysis of variance. The report, however, gives a different definition of the “core activity” of statistics. I reproduce the first two paragraphs of Section 4 of the original report so that readers do not have to locate it.
Statistics has an expanding intellectual heritage that we might, for lack of a better
Leo Breiman is Emeritus Professor, Department of Statistics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 947203860, USA (e-mail: leo@stat.Berkeley.edu).
word, call the core of statistics. This terminology is not routinely used in the profession and so it is necessary to define rather precisely what is meant. We will define the core of statistics as the subset of statistical activity that is focused inward on the subject itself, rather than outward towards the needs of statistics in particular scientific domains. As a synonym for ‘core’ the word ‘inreach’ might be offered. This would reflect the fact that this core activity is the opposite of outreach. As such, almost all statisticians are active in both inreach and outreach activities. The research in the core area is focused on the development of statistical models, methods, and related theory based on the general principles of the field. The objectives are to create unifying philosophies, concepts, statistical methods, and computational tools. Although this is introspective activity, a central philosophy of the core is that the importance of a problem is not dictated by its intrinsic beauty (as, say, in abstract mathematics). Rather, its importance is dictated by its potential for wide application or, alternatively, its value in expanding understanding of the scientific validity of our methods.
Would John Tukey agree that this is the core activity of statistics? Given all that I know about John, I doubt it. Furthermore, it denigrates the way that the most important advances in statistics have occurrednot by introspection, but involvement in challenging problems suggested by different disciplines and data. Furthermore (Section 3.4) the report urges that more funding be given activity that is focused inward rather than outward. At a time when statistics is beginning to recover from its “overmathematization” in the post World War II years and engage in significant applications in many areas, the report is a step into the past and not into the future.


