Comment
Vijay Nair
Lindsay, Kettenring and Siegmund have done the statistical community a great service by coordinating and editing this report. This is an important publication and I thank the Executive Editor for the opportunity to provide comments. I will restrict myself to general comments here because I have already provided detailed input to Section 4.2.
ENGINEERING AND INDUSTRIAL STATISTICS
The state of research in engineering and industrial statistics is not as healthy as it was two to three decades ago. The short-term focus in business and industry has led to drastic cutbacks in industry-based research. Within most statistics departments in academia, engineering and industrial statistics are viewed as mature areas and do not attract much interest. This is unfortunate because there is a wide range of important and interesting research problems, some of which are described in Section 4.2 (Section 5.2 of the original report). The report on “Visionary Manufacturing Challenges for 2020” (National Academy Press; available at http://www.nap.edu/readingroom/books/ visionary/ ) notes that “manufacturing will remain one of the principal means by which wealth is created [in the United States].” It goes on to identify one of the key challenges as “instantaneously transform[ing] information gathered from a vast array of diverse sources into useful knowledge for making effective decisions.” However, this requires a holistic approach to modeling, simulation, design, analysis, optimization and decision-making—something at which statisticians have not been particularly good. A case in point is the extensive work on statistical process control (monitoring; see Stoumbos, Reynolds, Ryan and Woodall, 2000, for a review). We have focused primarily on the hypothesis testing and changepoint testing problems, viewing the more interesting topics of failure diagnosis and feedback/feedforward control as the domain of engineers. However, in advanced manufacturing applications with complex
Vijay Nair is Professor, Department of Statistics and Department of Industrial and Operations Engineering, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109-1092, USA (e-mail: vnn@umich.edu).
processes, engineers themselves rely on statistical methods to develop and implement real-time diagnostics and control (Nair, Hansen and Shi, 2000, and references therein). Statistics has a major role to play here. In fact, the data have much more information when the process is out of control than in control. Even within the realm of process monitoring, the increasing complexity of manufacturing data (images, spatial objects, etc.) presents interesting challenges. New problems, such as fraud detection, network monitoring and early detection of warranty problems, that deal with large data bases and are often cited as problems in data mining, are in fact complex manifestations of process monitoring, and past research in statistical process control (SPC) has much to offer in addressing these problems. Another major research topic that was briefly touched on in the report is analysis of complex systems using modeling and simulation. This actually cuts across many areas of application—engineering (aircraft, automotive, semiconductor, nuclear, space), physics, biology, chemistry, epidemiology and meteorology. Design and analysis of computer experiments as well as model validation raise difficult technical challenges. Computational models represent a different type of “data”—codification of subject matter knowledge. In contrast to applications involving large data sets, data from computer models (function evaluations) are sparse since they can be very expensive to collect. The predominant issue is lack of model fit rather than random error. Research here has not kept pace with the needs of applications. It presents a fruitful opportunity for collaboration between statisticians and applied mathematicians. The drastic drop in industry-based research means that universities and national labs have to pick up the slack. We can learn here from the experiences and models of engineering disciplines. Statisticians need to tap more into funding opportunities within the engineering divisions of the NSF and programs such as GOALI, aimed at encouraging collaborations among industrial and academic partners. However, these are not sufficient to offset the loss. In the long run, business and industry will have to find a way to support research while also being competitive in the marketplace. Leaders in industry must also move beyond criticizing academia about curriculum and research relevance


