and work together to implement change and transfer of technology.
EDUCATION AND TRAINING
In view of the rapid changes in the field and the pipeline problem in attracting students, we must reexamine the source and mix of skills for incoming graduate students. There is no single major that can provide the right skills. What is needed is an interdisciplinary program, perhaps called informatics, that provides adequate training in mathematics, statistics and probability coupled with background in programming, data bases, optimization and numerical analysis. A minor in a subject matter area would be highly desirable. While this might take students away from purely statistics degree programs, the net effect would be to attract more students and expose them to statistics at the undergraduate and Master’s levels, and increase the pipeline of Ph.D. students. Business and industry have invested tremendous resources to (re)train the workforce extensively in basic statistical methods over the last two decades, mostly in quality and productivity improvement methods (see, e.g., the black-belt curriculum for Six-Sigma). Yet statistics and probability are not part of the core curriculum in engineering departments. It is time that our professional organizations work together with the Accreditation Board for Engineering and Technology to address this gap.
IDENTITY AND IMAGE OF THE PROFESSION
These are indeed great times for the discipline, but we have done a dismal job of conveying the excitement about and opportunities in our profession to the outside world. We are just a notch above accountants on the nerd scale. Our organizations (ASA, IMS, ISI,
etc.) have been talking about outreach for some time, but there has been little progress. Most of the interesting developments that deal with data and the information age are associated with computer science. With the boundaries with computer science blurring, we are in danger of losing our professional identity. Until we can get across the excitement we all share and the important contributions statistics makes, we will continue to face problems attracting good students and having our discipline fully appreciated. Ultimately, this may be the biggest challenge facing the profession!
